INT. BELL FOUNDRY - OCCUPIED TOWN - NIGHT

A cathedral of soot. Cold casting pits. Molds like sleeping giants. Moonlight through broken skylights.

Silence.

SERGEANT WOJCIECH NOWAK, 40, boots wrapped in sacking, crouches by the great furnace door. Rifle low. Grey in his stubble.

CORPORAL ESTELLE ROUSSEAU, 25, radio operator, frost on her eyelashes. Headset on. Pistol in her fist.

PRIVATE ANDERS HAUGEN, 19, freckled, too tall for the crouch. He keeps swallowing.

Wind. A loose chain taps a bell. Tick. Tick.

NOWAK
(whisper)
Count them again.

ROUSSEAU
Same as before. Six trucks. Engines off.

HAUGEN
Why would they park here?

NOWAK
Because they think we're dead.

Nowak lifts two fingers. Haugen creeps to the window. Rousseau tunes the dial. Static.

A boot crunches glass outside. Close.

All three stop breathing.

HAUGEN
(barely audible)
Sergeant.

NOWAK
I hear it.

A flashlight beam sweeps the molds. It stops on Haugen's shadow.

ROUSSEAU
(mouthing)
Don't. Move.

The beam holds. Holds. Moves on.

Haugen exhales. Too loud. The chain stops tapping.

Outside, doors slam. Engines cough alive.

NOWAK
They know.

ROUSSEAU
Radio's dead. No one is coming.

NOWAK
Then we make noise before they do.

He pulls a satchel charge from his pack. Looks at the great bell hanging above the furnace.

HAUGEN
Sergeant, the bell. Everyone will hear.

NOWAK
That's the idea.

He slings the charge over the bell's crown. Lights the fuse.

Rousseau and Haugen are already running.

The foundry doors burst inward. Flashlights stab.

The charge goes off. The bell shrieks. Fire floods the dark.
